course: dictionary
lesson: 8850
title: Shoe and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. «Put on your shoes before we leave.» Here shoe means the thing you wear on your foot.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Bring me a pair of shoes» tells you there are two, because a pair is 2 shoes. A horseshoe is different: it is a curved piece of metal fixed to a horse's hoof, so check the context before you decide.
explain: Some words are used in phrases with a special meaning. «Think how it feels to be in someone's shoes» does not mean wearing their footwear. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A shoe is something you wear on your foot.
fact: Each shoe has a firm bottom called the sole.
fact: Shoes come in pairs: a pair is 2 shoes.
fact: A horseshoe is a curved piece of metal fixed to a horse's hoof.
fact: «In someone's shoes» means in their situation.
exercise: «The stonefish looks like a lumpy stone, but reef shoes in shallow water protect your feet.» What do reef shoes protect? | Your ears | *Your feet | The sea floor | Your head
exercise: «Bring me a pair of shoes from the hall.» How many shoes are in a pair? | Four | One | Three | *Two
exercise: «Put on your shoes and tie your shoelaces before school.» What do you do to your shoelaces? | *Tie them | Paint them | Cut them | Eat them
test: «Food-factory workers often walk through a shoe-cleaning bath, so no germs get into the food.» Why do the workers walk through the bath? | To dry their hairnets | To make their shoes shine for the boss | To cool their feet down | *To keep germs out of the food
test: «Think how it feels to stand in someone's shoes.» What does «in someone's shoes» mean? | Taller than them | Behind them in a line | *In their situation | Wearing their clothes
exam: «Des chaussures marron» uses marron (brown), which never changes. Does marron change in the plural? | Only in France | Yes, always | Only with orange shoes | *No, never, not even in the plural
